#
# Three answers this dialect never gave, and so gave dash's.
#
# #3248 was the sixth axis where `dialect/ash` set no value and the POSIX
# preset's — dash's, every time — was taken in silence. These are what a
# sweep of the rest of that shape turned up, and they are here because the
# reason none of them was caught is the same reason #3248 was not: `core/`
# can only hold what every reference agrees on, `dash/` holds the answers of
# the shell this one is *not*, and until #3248 the `ash/` tier had three
# files and none of them came near these questions.
#
# Each block asks the discriminating case and then a control the wrong answer
# also passes, so a column that agrees by accident is visible as one that
# agrees on the control alone.

printf -- '--- an empty regex operand ---\n'

# 0 here and in ksh93; 2 in the three bash builds and 1 in zsh, each with a
# sentence on standard error. dash has no `[[ ]]` to ask it in.
[[ abc =~ "" ]]; printf 'empty regex -> %s\n' "$?"

# The two controls, which say the operator works at all.
[[ abc =~ b ]]; printf 'a regex that matches -> %s\n' "$?"
[[ abc =~ x ]]; printf 'a regex that does not -> %s\n' "$?"

printf -- '--- a process substitution as a condition operand ---\n'

# The discriminator, and the row that separates "performed" from "read as
# the word that was written": the left side is the text of the construct and
# the right side is what it became, so 1 is a shell that performed it and 0 a
# shell that did not. zsh refuses here with a sentence of its own; ksh93 and
# dash refuse while reading, before the file runs at all.
[[ "<(echo x)" == <(echo x) ]]; printf 'compared against its own text -> %s\n' "$?"

# The control that cannot decide it, and is here to say so: `-e` is true on a
# /dev/fd name and false on the literal word, so it is 0 either way in every
# column that parses the line.
[[ -e <(echo x) ]]; printf 'the file test -> %s\n' "$?"

# And the construct works outside a condition, which is what says the two
# rows above are about `[[ ]]`.
[[ $(cat <(echo x)) == x ]]; printf 'under an ordinary command -> %s\n' "$?"

printf -- '--- `.` looking in the current directory ---\n'

# PATH is searched first in every column, and where it has the name it wins:
# that half is unanimous and is the control.
mkdir -p elsewhere
printf 'printf "the PATH copy ran\\n"\n' > elsewhere/both.sh
printf 'printf "the current directory copy ran\\n"\n' > both.sh
PATH="$PWD/elsewhere:/usr/bin:/bin"
. both.sh

# The discriminator: a name PATH has not got. bash and this shell read the
# copy in the current directory; dash, zsh and ksh93 report not found.
printf 'printf "only here\\n"\n' > only.sh
. only.sh
printf 'after the fallback -> %s\n' "$?"

# The arithmetic block is last on purpose. A shell that refuses a
# name-shaped value ends the script at that line, so with it first a
# regression there would take the other two blocks' rows with it and
# one wrong answer would read as three.
printf -- '--- a name-shaped value in arithmetic ---\n'

# dash reads the value as a literal and stops at `Illegal number: y`. Six of
# the seven columns look the name up in turn.
y=5; x=y
printf 'one step:  %s\n' "$((x+1))"

# And it recurses as far as the values lead, which is what says the first row
# is a lookup and not one extra step.
y=z; z=7; x=y
printf 'two steps: %s\n' "$((x+1))"

# The control: a numeral, which every column adds to.
n=5
printf 'a numeral: %s\n' "$((n+1))"
